INSERT INTO mail_templates (slug, name, segment, position, subject, preview, body) VALUES (
  $tpl$a-2$tpl$, $tpl$A · Email 2 — The documents themselves$tpl$, $tpl$A$tpl$, 2,
  $tpl$The documents behind the image, and what they do not say$tpl$,
  $tpl$Cardinal Rivera's 1998 certification and the Virgen Peregrina, both published in full for you to read.$tpl$,
  $tpl$Dear @firstname,

Following up on the canvas I wrote to you about, because the documentation is the part that usually matters to a parish, and it is the part most sellers gesture at without showing.

Two Mexican certifications are preserved with the image.

The first is dated **December 12, 1998** and signed by **Cardinal Norberto Rivera Carrera, Archbishop Primate of Mexico**. It describes the associated print as a *"reproducción fiel del archivo digital del Sagrado Original"* — a **faithful reproduction of the digital archive of the Sacred Original**. The same document identifies **Digital Color, S.A. de C.V.** with the digital capture and reproduction.

The second records that the image known as the **Virgen Peregrina** was **blessed by Pope John Paul II on January 25, 1999**, and traveled throughout Mexico from January 1999 to April 2000 for the Great Jubilee.

Both are published in full, with the Spanish transcribed and translated:

[**Read the certifications →**](https://primosmaternos.com/authenticity?utm_source=gmail&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=parish-outreach-2026&utm_content=a-2)

The same page says what the documents do *not* establish, which I think matters as much. They do not mean that Cardinal Rivera examined any canvas printed since, that any canvas sold today was individually blessed, or that the Church endorses our business. Primos Maternos came under its current ownership in 2005, years after these events, and we present what survives rather than filling the gaps.

Our canvases are printed in the United States and ship rolled, at **$114 with free shipping to the 50 states and Puerto Rico**.

Thanks,
Peace Bill

William F. Leonard
Saxon Enterprises, Inc. — dba Primos Maternos
15 Shea Ln, Tinton Falls, NJ 07724
wfleonard@primosmaternos.com · 732-673-4260$tpl$
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO mail_templates (slug, name, segment, position, subject, preview, body) VALUES (
  $tpl$a-3$tpl$, $tpl$A · Email 3 — The image itself (value, not sales)$tpl$, $tpl$A$tpl$, 3,
  $tpl$What the Aztecs read in the Guadalupe image$tpl$,
  $tpl$Every fold, sash, and star was a message. A short read for your Guadalupe ministry or December 12 preparation.$tpl$,
  $tpl$Dear @firstname,

Not a sales email — just an article your Guadalupe ministry may find useful for catechesis, a bulletin insert, or December 12 preparation.

Spanish missionaries preached in Mexico for ten years before the tilma with almost no result. In the ten years after Juan Diego opened his cloak in front of the bishop, roughly nine million Aztecs were baptized. Historians of the image now understand why: to the Aztecs the image was a codex, written in symbols they read at a glance.

The mestiza face anticipating a race that didn't yet exist. Hair parted in the middle with no tie — an Aztec sign for a pregnant virgin. The black sash — the Aztec maternity belt. The four-petaled Nahui Ollin over the womb — the Aztec glyph for the presence of God. The stars on her mantle matching the actual sky over Mexico City on the morning of December 12, 1531.

Full breakdown with sources, in about seven minutes of reading:

[**Read: A Codex in the Image →**](https://primosmaternos.com/blog/codex-symbols?utm_source=gmail&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=parish-outreach-2026&utm_content=a-3)

For parishes and ministries that would like the image itself, we make a 24" × 36" rolled canvas — printed in the USA from the same 1998-certified digital archive of the Sacred Original — available for $114, with free U.S. shipping and both Mexican certifications.

[See the image →](https://primosmaternos.com/parishes?utm_source=gmail&utm_medium=email&utm_campaign=parish-outreach-2026&utm_content=a-3b)

Thanks,
Peace Bill

William F. Leonard
Saxon Enterprises, Inc. — dba Primos Maternos
15 Shea Ln, Tinton Falls, NJ 07724
wfleonard@primosmaternos.com · 732-673-4260$tpl$
) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;

